// Shadow IT (systems deployed by departments other than central IT; self developed spreadsheets, macros, and databases as
//   unofficial data flows; security and compliance risk): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadow_IT ; end user computing (risk
//   drivers; unmanaged EUC in finance exposes the organization): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/End-user_computing
// Capacity management (resources sufficient for upcoming business requirements, cost effectively; monitoring utilization and
//   throughput): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capacity_management ; service level agreement (metrics, penalties, regular review;
//   outsourcing managed through SLAs): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Service-level_agreement
// IT asset management (hardware and software, discovery compared to entitlements, life cycle from acquisition through disposal):
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_center_management ; end of life: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Product_lifecycle
// Dates: Tuesday, March 23 and Wednesday, March 24, 2027. US daylight saving started March 14 and the UK changes Sunday, March 28,
//   so Denver is UTC minus 6 and six hours behind London: 2 PM in Denver is 8 PM in London. In April 2026, when Henry moved, both
//   countries were on summer time, seven hours apart, and from March 28, 2027 they are seven apart again (Arthur's watch). In
//   February and early March Denver was on standard time, UTC minus 7: 2027-02-27 06:14 UTC is Friday, February 26, 11:14 PM
//   MST; 2027-03-08 16:02 UTC is Monday, March 8, 9:02 AM MST. Weekdays computed: Tuesday, March 2; Wednesday, March 3;
//   Thursday, March 4; Monday, March 1 and Friday, March 12 (leave); Saturday, March 27; Monday, March 29.
// Figures (drill, re-derived): rule SC-04 off from 2027-02-27 06:14 UTC to 2027-03-08 16:02 UTC = 9 days (February 2027 has 28
//   days) + 9 h 48 m = 216 + 9.8 = 225.8 hours = 13,548 minutes; 13,548 / 41 = 330.4, about 330 times the longest ticketed
//   window. DOME rule configuration history retained 1 year; admin audit trail (sign ins, session source) retained 28 days: February 27 + 28 days = March 27, 06:14 UTC = 12:14 AM MDT Saturday; from
//   Tuesday, March 23 that's 4 days. Raw events retained 30 days: March 29, 06:14 UTC. DOME capacity: license 60 GB a day, average
//   57.2, peak 64.0, 19 of 59 days over (January 31 + February 28 = 59); February 26 was 44.1 GB. Log feed February 27 UTC: 41,288
//   sent, 41,288 received. Service desk tickets January 1 to March 5: 206. Operating system unpatched since September 2023 (three
//   and a half years by March 2027).
// Lab (bible ch8 plan, "What DOME saw", not built in labs.js yet; whoever builds it should match these): dome_alert_rules.csv holds
//   rule SC-04 "Settlement components: change outside a declared release window", 58 ticketed windows opened by pellridge-deploy
//   with a CHG number and closed 12 to 41 minutes later, one DISABLED 2027-02-27 06:14 UTC (exported as 2027-02-26 23:14 MST) by
//   pellridge-deploy with a blank reason, ENABLED 2027-03-08 16:02 UTC with reason CHG-2207; dome_alerts.csv has no SC-04 alerts in
//   that window; ticket_log.csv has no ticket for February 26. Same minute as the unticketed 4.11.2 release in the ch6 deploy log.
//   Rule history is kept a year; DOME sign ins only 28 days. Henry finds it from Meridian's export through counsel, never by signing in.
// Names: "Beth Nyland" (B.N., settlement supervisor, she/her) checked 2026-10-16 against every BIBLE*.md in the repo, the canon
//   folder, and every course .js file: no "Nyland" and no "Beth" anywhere. She never appears on the page. No new speaker; the
//   lawyer from Meridian's legal team and the night lead are unnamed, they/them, and don't speak. "Alan Mercado" (the police
//   department's lawyer, named only, no pronouns) checked the same way: no "Mercado" and no "Alan" anywhere.
// Facts set here for later chapters: Teresa sent Lou the W/P 17 request in writing Tuesday, March 23, 9 AM, copied to Alan Mercado, the police
//   department's lawyer, for examination with that lawyer and Teresa present (ch11's W/P 17 in full follows from it). Service desk tickets Tuesday, March 2, 8:12 AM; Wednesday, March 3,
//   7:58 AM; Thursday, March 4, 8:05 AM: approvers report a file showing as approved before they approved it; closed by Pellridge's
//   desk in 6, 4, and 9 minutes as display cache, no fault found; no problem record, no linked change, no user confirmation.
//   Pellridge's February service report: availability 99.98 percent (target 99.95), incidents resolved within SLA 100 percent
//   (resolved = closed by Pellridge's desk), measured by Pellridge, "Noted" by the CIO on March 2. Interface register lists six
//   interfaces (4 is release service to DOME, the log feed); the nightly settlement file feed to Pellridge's archive isn't on it.
//   B.N. is Beth Nyland, day shift settlement supervisor (Meridian's general counsel's letter, Tuesday, March 23); the sign off
//   sheets print from workbook RECON_SIGNOFF on the settlement share, built 2019 by Longitude's project team, not on the
//   application inventory, free text initials, no version history, 11 people can open it (9 settlement, the 2 Pellridge night
//   operators). Queue log: bnyland carried the March 2 and March 3 breaks forward at 7:44 AM March 3 and 7:39 AM March 4 from
//   workstation SR-N1; HR: Beth Nyland (day shift, 8 AM to 4:30 PM) on approved leave Monday, March 1 to Friday, March 12, no remote access; asset register:
//   SR-N1 is a shared night desk workstation, assigned to nobody, no custodian, last inventory check June 2024. Who used the ID
//   is open (ch9). Release service runs 4.11.2 on two virtual servers whose operating system lost vendor support October 2023, no
//   extended support, last patched September 2023. Night visit Tuesday 10 PM to after midnight, ninth floor, observe only, a
//   lawyer from Meridian's legal team present; Leonard offered DOME on screen or internal audit's shared read only login (AUDIT_RO,
//   kept in the night lead's drawer; a ch9 access line) at 11:50 PM. Leonard: Ms. Kessler never touched that drawer in eight years. loggedin path: session 12:41 AM Wednesday under AUDIT_RO, in DOME's audit trail; Meridian's general counsel objects
//   Wednesday; Nkechi tells him at 3 PM. DOME export arrived Wednesday 7:10 AM via Meridian's counsel; raw event export at 5 PM.
//   Teresa sent the bureau the queue log, leave record, and rule history Wednesday 2:40 PM on her own call. Nkechi came at 3:01;
//   her car across Wazee had an inch of undisturbed snow on the windshield though it had been snowing only since about one (her
//   ch8 tell, unexplained). Maisie: Laura kept the London slide, 45:40, cut an ozone joke; Maisie says "you tell her", Henry texts Laura, Laura: "45:40. I'm keeping the forty seconds too." Arthur set his watch seven hours back
//   ("it was seven when you went"); Henry: leave it, it'll be right on Sunday. Heard path (all[1].picks[7]===2): Henry drafts and
//   deletes an email to Priyanka about the 1994 slip; nothing confirmed.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cisa=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa.full||[]).push({
  num: 8, part: `Part Two: The Dome`, title: `Night Shift`, weeks: `Tuesday, March 23 to Wednesday, March 24`,
  tasks: [`4A1`,`4A2`,`4A4`,`4A5`,`4A6`,`4A7`,`4A9`,`4A10`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `Lou had told him how to ask: in writing, through counsel, to him. Henry wrote it before he went home on Monday night, three paragraphs with no adjectives in them, and at nine on Tuesday Teresa Vreeland sent it to Lou on her own letterhead, copied to Alan Mercado, the department's lawyer. It asked that the department examine the second sheet in the MC folder, the one marked W/P 17, with the department's lawyer and Teresa present, and photograph it front and back. He filed his copy and didn't expect an answer this week.`,
    `The rest of Tuesday had been arranged by other people. Leonard Pratt had invited the insurer's investigator to watch Meridian's settlement run that night, from the build at ten to the release at half past eleven, and Teresa had agreed on terms she wrote herself. Observe only. Touch nothing. No photographs. Somebody from Meridian's legal team in the room the whole time. Henry hadn't asked to go. He found that he wanted to.`,
    `And there was the question he'd carried since Friday. Two of Meridian's own records disagreed about the night of February 26. The deployment tool had a release nobody had ticketed, going into the service that sends out the money. DOME, which watched every component and fed every timeline in the claim, had nothing at all. First thing on Tuesday he asked Teresa for DOME's alert rules and their history. He wanted to know which one to believe, and why, before anyone at Meridian offered to tell him.`,
    `He wrote DOME on the whiteboard, under the shopping list and the years, put a question mark after it, and went to make the coffee.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Dot's Workpapers: the night shift`,
    sections: [
      { h: `What it's made of`,
        body: [`Before you audit a system, Lime Street, find out what it's made of. Every application sits on a pile of other things: hardware, or the virtual machines that pretend to be hardware; an operating system; a database; middleware that carries files and messages between programs; the network that joins it all; and these days, as often as not, somebody else's data center. Each layer can fail, and each needs its own controls. A perfect application on an operating system nobody patches is a perfect application with the door off.`,
          `Every component has a life. The maker supports it for a while, with fixes for the faults people find in it, and then announces a date after which there won't be any more. Past that date, every new weakness found in it stays open for good. Companies run unsupported software for reasons. The upgrade's dear, the vendor's application only runs on the old version, nobody wants to touch a thing that works. Sometimes that's a fair risk, if management takes it knowingly, in writing, and puts something round the old system to make up for it. When I find it with no decision on file, nobody took the risk. It just happened to them.`,
          `You can't look after what you don't know you have, so the asset inventory comes first. It lists hardware and software: what each thing is, where it lives, who owns it, which version it runs, what license covers it, and when it's due to be replaced. Every asset needs a named owner, a person and not a department, because a department never answers the phone. The inventory follows each asset from the purchase order to disposal, and disposal means the data is wiped or the disk destroyed, with a certificate to prove it.`,
          `Then test the list against the world. Pick from the inventory and go and find the thing. Then go the other way: take what's actually on the network, or on the floor, and look for it in the list. Shared machines that belong to everyone and nobody are where I'd start. Nobody notices when one is used at an odd hour, because nobody thinks of it as theirs.`],
        terms: [[`IT components`,`The layers a system depends on: hardware or virtual machines, operating system, database, middleware, network, and any hosting provider.`],[`End of support`,`The date after which a vendor stops issuing fixes for a product. Weaknesses found later stay unpatched.`],[`Asset inventory`,`A record of hardware and software assets: what, where, version, license, owner, and life cycle status.`],[`Asset owner`,`The named person accountable for an asset and its protection.`]],
        exam: `Unsupported software can't be patched, so its risk must be formally accepted or reduced with compensating controls. A complete, accurate asset inventory with named owners underpins security, licensing, monitoring, and disposal. Test completeness by tracing from what exists back to the inventory.` },
      { h: `Where two systems meet`,
        body: [`An interface is where one system hands data to another: a file dropped in a folder at night, a message on a queue, one program calling another. Most of the trouble I've seen in payments lives there, because each side assumes the other side is checking.`,
          `So each interface should be written down: what goes across, how often, in what format, who owns each end, and what happens when something goes wrong. Then the controls. Control totals travel with each file, a record count and an amount, and get checked on arrival, so nothing is lost or added on the way. Sequence numbers make a missing file or a duplicate stand out. Rejected records go to a queue somebody owns, and they're fixed and resubmitted, not quietly dropped. Data in transit is encrypted, over connections where each end proves who it is. And now and again somebody reconciles the whole chain, from the first system to the last.`,
          `Keep an inventory of interfaces as you would of assets, and look for the ones that aren't on it. A feed somebody set up for a project and never switched off. A copy of every file going to a vendor because it was convenient in 2019. An undocumented interface is data leaving by a door nobody's watching, and nobody's counting what goes out.`],
        terms: [[`System interface`,`A point where data passes from one system to another, by file, message, or direct call.`],[`Interface control totals`,`Record counts and amounts sent with the data and checked on arrival to show it came across complete and unchanged.`],[`Interface inventory`,`The list of interfaces with their owners, data, frequency, and controls.`]],
        exam: `Interface controls show data passed completely and accurately between systems: record counts, control totals, sequence checks, error handling with resubmission, and periodic reconciliation. An undocumented interface carrying sensitive data is a risk to integrity and confidentiality.` },
      { h: `The spreadsheet in the drawer`,
        body: [`Shadow IT is any system a department runs without IT knowing about it or approving it: a cloud service bought on a company card, a database somebody built on a weekend, a sharing site full of customer files. End user computing is its quieter cousin, the spreadsheets, macros, and little databases people build to get their own work done. I've no quarrel with any of it in principle. People build these things because the official systems don't do what they need.`,
          `The trouble is what happens next. The spreadsheet starts carrying a control. Then it's carrying money. And it's still a spreadsheet: no real access control, no record of who changed what, no testing, no backup but luck, and one person who understands it. When that person leaves, nobody does.`,
          `What the company should have is an inventory of the ones that matter, rated by what would happen if each one were wrong, with controls in proportion. For a spreadsheet behind a key control that means locked cells, access only for the people who need it, versions kept, changes tested and approved, inputs checked, a backup, and a second person who knows how it works. For cloud services, a rule about who may buy them, a review before anyone puts company data in one, and some way of finding the ones nobody mentioned.`,
          `When you audit, ask the people who do the work what they actually use, and compare it with what IT thinks they use. And whenever a sign off is a name typed into a cell, remember that anyone who can open the file can type.`],
        terms: [[`Shadow IT`,`Technology used or bought by a business unit without IT's knowledge or approval.`],[`End user computing (EUC)`,`Applications built and run by users themselves, such as spreadsheets, macros, and desktop databases.`],[`EUC inventory`,`A risk rated list of the user built tools that support important processes, so controls can be applied in proportion.`]],
        exam: `The main risk of end user computing is that important data and controls sit outside IT's general controls: access, change and version control, testing, and backup. The first step in managing it is a risk rated inventory.` },
      { h: `Enough room, and promises about it`,
        body: [`Availability is whether a system is there when the business needs it. Capacity is whether it has room to do the work: processing power, memory, storage, network bandwidth, and licenses, which people forget are capacity too. Capacity management watches how much of each is used, follows the trend, sets thresholds that warn well before the limit, and plans from the business's own forecasts, so the company buys what it needs before it runs out. A system run at its limit every night doesn't usually break. It starts quietly doing less: queuing, delaying, dropping things.`,
          `When the work goes to a vendor, the promises go into a service level agreement. A good one says what's measured, how, by whom, how often it's reported, and what happens when a target's missed. The targets come from what the business needs, not from what the vendor finds easy. Inside IT, operational agreements between teams hold the promise up.`,
          `Then read the definitions, because that's where the cleverness hides. If resolved means the vendor closed the ticket, a vendor can resolve anything in four minutes. If availability is measured by the vendor's own tool, from the vendor's side, it measures what the vendor chooses to see. The customer should get the data underneath, measure something itself now and then, and review the reports with somebody who'll ask questions. A report that's hit every target for three years is describing either an excellent vendor or a soft measure. Find out which.`],
        terms: [[`Capacity management`,`Monitoring use of IT resources, forecasting demand from business plans, and planning so resources stay sufficient.`],[`Service level agreement (SLA)`,`An agreement between a provider and its customer on measurable service targets, how they're measured and reported, and remedies.`],[`Operational level agreement (OLA)`,`An internal agreement between teams that supports the service levels promised to the customer.`]],
        exam: `Capacity planning uses utilization trends and business forecasts to anticipate needs. Service levels should be defined from business requirements and measured with data the customer can verify. A provider's self reported results alone are weak evidence.` },
      { h: `When it breaks, and when it keeps breaking`,
        body: [`An incident is anything that interrupts a service or makes it worse. Incident management is about getting the service back as fast as possible. Log it, classify it, set a priority by impact and urgency, fix it or work round it, escalate it if it's beyond the first person or past its time, and close it only when the person who reported it agrees it's fixed. Speed is the point. Understanding comes later.`,
          `A problem is the underlying cause of one or more incidents, usually unknown when it's first recorded, and problem management is the later part. Somebody looks at incidents together, sees the same thing happening again, opens a problem record, gives it an owner, and goes looking for the root cause. When they've found the cause and have a workaround but no fix yet, that's a known error, and it's written down so the service desk stops rediscovering it. The permanent fix goes through change management like any other change.`,
          `The first question in any root cause analysis is what changed. Most incidents follow a change, and a good process links them, so a cluster of new tickets can be traced back to last Tuesday's release. When I look at a service desk I look for repeats. The same complaint three times, closed three times as no fault found, tells me the desk is measured on closing tickets and nobody is paid to ask why.`],
        terms: [[`Incident`,`An unplanned interruption to a service, or a reduction in its quality.`],[`Problem`,`The underlying cause of one or more incidents, usually unknown when the problem is recorded.`],[`Known error`,`A problem whose root cause is known and has a documented workaround, pending a permanent fix.`],[`Root cause analysis`,`Working back from an incident to the underlying cause, often starting with recent changes.`]],
        exam: `Incident management restores normal service quickly. Problem management finds and removes the root causes of recurring incidents. Incidents should be prioritized, escalated, and closed only when the user confirms. Trend analysis of incidents is how problems are found.` },
      { h: `The record of the night`,
        body: [`Logs are a system's memory of what happened, and in my line of work they're usually the best witness there is, so long as nobody's coached them. Decide what to log: sign ins, failed and successful, privileged actions, changes to configuration and security settings, and above all changes to the logging itself. Collect them in one place, away from the systems that wrote them, so the person who runs a system can't edit its history. Protect them from change, with access restricted and their integrity checked.`,
          `Clocks matter more than people think. Every system stamps its entries with its own clock. If the clocks disagree, or one writes UTC and another local time, you can't put events in order, and the order is the whole case. Every system should take its time from the same source. When you compare logs, convert everything to one zone first, and check the time source before you trust the minute.`,
          `Retention is how long logs are kept as a matter of course, set by policy and sometimes by law. Preservation is keeping what would otherwise be thrown away, because something in it matters now. When a log might become evidence, find out how long it's going to live, then ask in writing, through the proper channel, for it to be kept, with a hash taken when it's copied. Don't wait for the routine export.`,
          `Two rules about people. Whoever is being monitored shouldn't control the monitoring. An account that can switch off the alerts about its own work is a fox with a key to the henhouse. In an ordinary audit you'll often have a read only login and pull what you need yourself, and that's fine. But when the logs may end up as evidence, you get them as copies, exported and preserved by somebody accountable, and you don't go and browse the live system yourself because it's quicker. Your session would be in the evidence too, and you'd spend a morning in court explaining it.`],
        terms: [[`Log retention`,`Keeping logs for a set period as routine, under policy or regulation.`],[`Log preservation`,`Keeping specific logs beyond routine retention because they're relevant to an incident or investigation.`],[`Time synchronization`,`Setting every system's clock from a common source so log entries can be put in order.`],[`Log integrity`,`Protection of logs against change, shown by controls such as restricted access and hashing.`]],
        exam: `Logs should be centrally collected, protected from change, time synchronized, retained under policy, and reviewed. Administrators and monitored users shouldn't be able to alter logs or the monitoring of their own actions. Obtain log evidence as preserved, verifiable copies.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f8s1`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4A7`, title:`No fault found`, q:`Henry is reading the service desk tickets from the week before the event. What should he make of them?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The service desk export had come in Monday's batch, and he'd left it, because Monday had been about getting things back. It held two hundred and six tickets raised by Meridian's staff since January and closed by Pellridge's service desk. He sorted them by date and started with the week before the event.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Service desk tickets, settlement release service, March 1 to 5 (extract)">Tue Mar 2, 8:12 AM. Raised by: settlement approver. "File showed as approved when I opened it. I hadn't approved it." Closed 8:18 AM by Pellridge service desk: display cache, no fault found.<br>Wed Mar 3, 7:58 AM. Raised by: settlement approver. Same description. Closed 8:02 AM: no fault found.<br>Thu Mar 4, 8:05 AM. Raised by: settlement approver. Same description. Closed 8:14 AM: no fault found.<br>Problem record: none. Linked change: none. User confirmed fix: not recorded.</prop>`,`Three mornings running, three different approvers by their user IDs, and the same complaint in nearly the same words.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ring the approvers who raised them and ask what they saw.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He'd be ringing the insured's staff at work, in the middle of a fraud claim, about the week before the event, and whatever they told him would reach their managers before lunch. Their tickets are in writing, made at the time. That's better than anything they'd say to him on the phone.`,
          why:`Contact with the auditee's staff goes through the agreed channel. Records made at the time are stronger evidence than recollection, and direct contact can alert the people involved.`},
        {t:`Accept the service desk's three closures as no fault found.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He notes three display faults, resolved. The people who decided there was no fault were the people whose system it was, and nobody asked the approvers whether the fix had fixed anything.`,
          why:`An incident should be closed only when the user confirms service is restored. Closure by the provider alone, of repeats especially, isn't evidence that the cause was dealt with.`},
        {t:`Look at the three as one problem rather than three incidents.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Three tickets, three mornings, one complaint, each closed by the vendor in under ten minutes as a display fault, and nobody checked the fix with the person who'd raised it. One of those is an incident. Three in a row is a problem, and a problem gets its own record, an owner, and somebody looking for the cause, which usually starts with what changed. Nobody opened one. The Friday before, something had changed. He puts the ticket dates in a column beside the release on February 26 and doesn't draw a line between them yet.`,
          why:`Recurring incidents should trigger problem management: a problem record, an owner, and root cause analysis that starts with recent changes. Closing each repeat as "no fault found" without the user's confirmation treats a problem as a string of unrelated incidents.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel for any root cause work behind the closures.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Teresa sends it. If there's any, it'll matter. The export says what there is: no problem record, no link to any change, and nobody asked the person who complained.`,
          why:`Asking is reasonable. The record shows the finding now: repeats closed without root cause analysis or the user's confirmation.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s2`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4A10`, title:`Four pages of green`, q:`Pellridge's February service report is green on every figure. What should Henry do with it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Pellridge's February service report was four pages long, and every figure on it was green.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Pellridge service report to Meridian, February 2027 (extract)">Availability, settlement platform: 99.98 percent (target 99.95).<br>Incidents resolved within service level: 100 percent (target 95). Resolved: ticket closed by the Pellridge service desk.<br>Measured by: Pellridge monitoring. Reviewed by Meridian: "Noted," CIO, March 2.</prop>`,`March's report hadn't been written yet. When it was, the three tickets from the first week would count toward its hundred percent. By that definition each of them had been resolved inside ten minutes.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Check who defines each measure and who supplies the data.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The definitions go down before the numbers, because the numbers come out of them. Resolved means Pellridge's desk closed the ticket, so the hundred percent measures how quickly Pellridge closes things. Availability is measured by Pellridge's own monitoring, from Pellridge's side. Meridian's review is one word from the CIO. Nobody at Meridian has the data underneath, and the contract gives it no right to ask. The report isn't false. Nothing in it was ever checked by anyone it could disappoint.`,
          why:`Service level results are only as good as the definitions and data behind them. Metrics the provider defines, measures, and reports itself, reviewed without the underlying data, give little assurance. The customer should be able to verify performance.`},
        {t:`Take the report as evidence that Pellridge met its service levels.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He notes service levels met. They were met the way a student meets a deadline they set themselves, and the three tickets he read an hour ago are about to count toward the hundred percent.`,
          why:`A provider's own report, built on its own definitions, isn't independent evidence of performance.`},
        {t:`Sample ten February closures and trace each one in the export.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Eight of the ten were closed inside a quarter of an hour, and four of those were reopened by the same person the next day as new tickets. It's useful. But the definition at the bottom of the page is the finding, and it doesn't need a sample to be wrong.`,
          why:`Testing the underlying data is worthwhile. The weakness is how "resolved" is defined and who measures it, which the report itself shows.`},
        {t:`Recommend Meridian withhold fees, audit the vendor and reset targets.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`It's Meridian's contract and Meridian's money, and a list of remedies isn't a finding. He hasn't written the finding.`,
          why:`Record the condition first. Commercial remedies are management's decision, not the auditor's.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s3`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4A4`, title:`Six arrows`, q:`Meridian's interface register lists six owned feeds. What should Henry do about the Pellridge archive feed?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Meridian's interface register came at eleven, in a batch Teresa had chased twice.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian interface register, settlement (extract)">1. Merchant portal to settlement application. 2. Settlement application to release service. 3. Release service to Federal Reserve gateway. 4. Release service to DOME (log feed). 5. Settlement application to general ledger. 6. Bank returns to reconciliation.<br>Each: owner named; record counts and control totals checked on arrival; errors to an owned queue.</prop>`,`Six arrows, each with an owner. He took out the Pellridge invoices from the week before and found the line he'd circled: <b>Settlement file archive, seven year retention: $8,400.</b> Every night since 2019 a copy of every settlement file had gone to Pellridge's archive, and in March that copy was what got Meridian back. It wasn't on the register.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Leave the archive feed off as a recovery service, not an interface.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It is a recovery service, and it saved the company in March. It also takes a copy of every settlement file Meridian sends, every night, to a vendor's store that no register lists and nobody at Meridian owns. Saving the company once doesn't put it on the register.`,
          why:`An interface that carries data needs to be documented, owned, and controlled, whatever its value in an emergency.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel for the feed's design and its transfer logs.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Teresa adds it to Friday's list. The design will show how it works. That nobody at Meridian listed it, owns it, or checks what goes down it is on the page in front of him.`,
          why:`Asking for the design is reasonable. The finding is that the interface isn't inventoried or controlled.`},
        {t:`Write up the missing register entry for Meridian himself.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`He'd be documenting Meridian's interface for it, from Pellridge's invoices, and his entry would become the only record that the feed exists.`,
          why:`Keeping the interface inventory is management's job. The auditor reports the gap.`},
        {t:`Treat the archive feed as a seventh interface and test it like one.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`A seventh arrow, drawn in by hand. Every night since 2019, a copy of every settlement file has left Meridian for Pellridge's archive by a route no register lists. Nobody at Meridian owns it. Nobody counts what goes down it or checks that what arrives is what was sent, and nobody would notice if it stopped, or if something else went with it. The six listed interfaces each have totals and an owner. The one carrying everything has neither.`,
          why:`Every interface should be inventoried, owned, and controlled with counts, totals, and error handling. An undocumented feed of complete settlement data to a vendor is a risk to confidentiality and integrity, whatever its use in recovery.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s4`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4A5`, title:`Typed`, q:`Meridian names Beth Nyland as the B.N. on the reconciliation sheets. What should Henry do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At one Teresa forwarded Meridian's answer to the question Giles had asked on Friday.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Letter, Meridian's general counsel to Vellacott's counsel, Tuesday, March 23 (extract)">The initials B.N. on the reconciliation sign off sheets for March 3 and 4 are those of Beth Nyland, settlement supervisor. The sheets are printed from the workbook RECON_SIGNOFF, which the settlement team keeps on its shared drive. A copy of the workbook is enclosed.</prop>`,`He opened the copy. According to its properties, the workbook had been built in 2019 by Longitude's project team. It wasn't on Meridian's application inventory. The initials went into a cell anyone could type in, and version history had never been switched on for that share. Eleven people could open it: nine in settlement operations and the two Pellridge operators on the night roster.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Write Beth Nyland into the finding as the preparer of record.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`He'd be naming a person on the strength of two letters typed into a cell that eleven people could type in. Meridian's letter says whose initials they are. It doesn't say who typed them.`,
          why:`Typed initials in an uncontrolled spreadsheet don't show who performed a control. Corroborate with system records before attributing an action to anyone.`},
        {t:`Check what controls the workbook has over who enters initials.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`A key detective control signs off in a spreadsheet that IT doesn't know exists. It has no access control beyond the share, no record of who changed what, no locked cells, and no review. The vendor's project team built it in 2019 and never handed it to anyone. So the sheet proves that two letters were typed. Who typed them has to come from a record that knows who did things, and the reconciliation queue keeps one. He writes down which records those are, and in what order he'll want them.`,
          why:`User built tools that support a key control need the controls any system would have: an inventory entry, access control, change and version control, and review. A typed name in a spreadsheet isn't evidence of who performed the control.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel for the queue log of who carried each break.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's the right record, and Teresa sends the request. But the sheet is a finding in itself, and he's stepped straight past it to chase the name.`,
          why:`The system log is the right evidence of who acted. The uncontrolled spreadsheet carrying a key control's sign off is a weakness in its own right.`},
        {t:`Work out who typed them from the workbook's save dates himself.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The workbook keeps no history, so all he'd be reading is when somebody last saved it, and guessing the rest. He'd be inventing an audit trail the file never kept, and signing his name to the guess.`,
          why:`Don't reconstruct a missing audit trail by inference. Report that the tool keeps none, and get the evidence from records that do.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s5`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4A6`, title:`Room`, q:`Pellridge's capacity report covers DOME's volumes for January and February. What should Henry make of it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const heard=((all&&all[1]&&all[1].picks)||{})[7]===2;
        return [`Precious rang at two, eight in the evening in London. He could hear a tea trolley somewhere behind her.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Precious">"He had a good day. He asked me what the time was where you are, so I told him, and he set his watch by it."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Which way?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Precious">"Back seven hours. I said six, love, your clocks have gone forward. He said seven. He said it was seven when you went."</span>`,`It had been. In April both countries were on summer time, and he'd flown out seven hours behind his father. On Sunday London's clocks would go forward too, and it would be seven again.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Leave it. It'll be right on Sunday."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Precious">"That's what I told him."</span>`,
        (heard
          ?`He didn't write it down. When she'd rung off he opened a new email to Priyanka, typed <b>The 1994 slip</b> in the subject line, looked at it for a while, and deleted it. Nothing on the claim needed it. He didn't pretend to himself that this was the reason.`
          :`He didn't write it down. There were some things he'd rather not see in his own hand.`),
        `At three he went back to the question on the whiteboard. If DOME had been too busy to look on February 26, Pellridge's capacity report would say so.`,`<prop type="doc" title="IT capacity report, January and February 2027 (Pellridge, extract)">DOME: licensed volume 60 GB a day. Average 57.2 GB; peak 64.0 GB. Days over the licensed volume: 19 of 59. On those days indexing was deferred and alerts delayed by up to 6 hours.<br>Friday, February 26: 44.1 GB. Not deferred.<br>License unchanged since 2019. Capacity plan last updated 2021. Assessment: "Adequate."</prop>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Conclude DOME was too swamped that night to notice anything.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It's a neat explanation, and the same page rules it out. February 26 was one of the quiet days, forty four gigabytes against a limit of sixty, and nothing was deferred.`,
          why:`Test an explanation against the evidence before adopting it. The capacity record shows that day wasn't affected.`},
        {t:`Accept Pellridge's assessment that capacity is adequate.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He writes "capacity adequate (Pellridge)." The same report says DOME ran past its limit on nineteen days in two months and held back its alerts for hours each time.`,
          why:`A capacity assessment has to be tested against utilization data. Regularly breaching a limit, with delayed processing, isn't adequate, whatever the summary says.`},
        {t:`Check the 26th against the days DOME ran over its limit.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Two findings, pointing different ways. DOME is too small for Meridian: on nineteen days out of fifty nine it took in more than it was licensed for and held back its alerts for up to six hours, under a license nobody has looked at since 2019 and a plan last touched in 2021. But February 26 wasn't one of those days. It was quiet. Whatever kept DOME from noticing that release, it wasn't room. He crosses one explanation off the whiteboard and puts the date beside the line.`,
          why:`Capacity management tracks use against limits and business growth and plans ahead. Routinely running past capacity, with delayed alerts, is a monitoring weakness. Ruling an explanation in or out takes that specific day's data.`},
        {t:`Ask Leonard why the 2021 capacity plan was never updated.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Leonard would answer by return, helpfully, and it would be internal audit's account of somebody else's plan. The report says what matters at the bottom of the page: a license from 2019 and a plan untouched since 2021.`,
          why:`The reason is worth knowing, but get it from the plan's owner through the agreed channel. The report shows the plan is stale, and the finding is the regular overload.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s6`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4A9`, title:`Twenty eight days`, q:`DOME's export is promised for Friday, and its logs roll off on different schedules. What should Henry do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Meridian's general counsel answered Teresa's morning request at a quarter to five. DOME's alert rules and their history would be exported and sent by Friday.`,`Friday was near enough to make him look up how long DOME kept things. The logging standard was in the IT policy pack he'd read on the twelfth for other reasons.`,`<prop type="doc" title="DOME logging standard (extract)">Rule configuration history: retained 1 year.<br>Administrator audit trail (sign ins, session source): retained 28 days, rolling.<br>Raw events from monitored systems: retained 30 days, rolling.<br>Alerts: retained 1 year.</prop>`,`He did the sum on the back of the capacity report. Twenty eight days from the early hours of February 27, UTC, was the early hours of Saturday, March 27, a few minutes after midnight in Denver. The rule history would keep for a year. But the record of who had signed in to DOME that night, and from where, would be gone before anyone in Denver woke up on Saturday.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Have counsel ask today that those records be kept and hashed.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`Teresa's letter goes at 5:20, and it isn't about speed. It asks Meridian to stop DOME's rolling deletion of the administrator audit trail and the raw events, to export everything they still hold now, with a hash taken at export, to leave the originals untouched, and to confirm in writing when that's done. Because the same records sit in the bureau's case, she copies Agent Obi. Retention is what a system keeps as routine. Preservation is keeping what it would otherwise throw away, because something in it matters, and somebody has to ask for it.`,
          why:`When logs may become evidence, check their retention and request preservation in writing, through the proper channel, with integrity controls, before routine deletion. Waiting for a routine export risks losing them.`},
        {t:`Wait for the export Meridian's counsel has promised for Friday.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`If it comes on Friday, it arrives the day before the February entries roll off. If it slips by a day, it arrives after.`,
          why:`A promised export isn't preservation. When routine deletion is close, request preservation in writing now.`},
        {t:`Ask Leonard to copy the audit trail tonight, hash it, and keep it.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Leonard would do it, and do it well, hash and all. It would still be a copy made informally by the insured's own staff, with no letter saying what was kept, on whose authority, or who holds it.`,
          why:`Keeping the records is right, but preservation should be requested through the proper channel, documented, and protected with integrity controls.`},
        {t:`Ask Halverson to image DOME this week, before anything rolls off.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Halverson is Meridian's firm, and it built the claim's timeline from DOME. He'd be asking the authors of the timeline to collect the evidence that tests it.`,
          why:`Evidence should be collected by someone independent of the conclusions it may test, through the agreed channel.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s7`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4A1`, title:`The ninth floor`, q:`Leonard's printed sheet shows vendor support for the operating system ended in 2023. What should Henry make of it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The ninth floor of the tower on Seventeenth Street was quieter than a room with eleven people working in it had any right to be. A lawyer from Meridian's legal team met him at the lift, signed him in, and sat at the back of the settlement room for the rest of the night with a laptop and their coat on. Leonard had stayed late to host. The night lead, a Pellridge engineer, walked him round once and went back to work.`,`Most of one wall was DOME: forty screens' worth of tiles, every one of them green. At ten the build started, and nothing on the wall changed. Near the window was a desk with nobody's name on it and a small label on the monitor, SR-N1. The night lead called it the night desk.`,`Leonard handed him a sheet run off for the visit.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Settlement release service: components (printed for the visit, Tuesday, March 23)">Application: release service, version 4.11.2 (Pellridge).<br>Servers: 2, virtual, Meridian data center.<br>Operating system: vendor support ended October 2023. Extended support: not purchased. Last patches applied: September 2023.<br>Database: shared with the settlement application. File transfer to the Federal Reserve gateway: vendor middleware, current version.</prop>`,`Version 4.11.2, printed by Meridian itself. He folded the sheet once and put it in his inside pocket.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask counsel for the upgrade plan and any risk acceptance.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Teresa will send it tomorrow. The sheet says what matters: nobody has patched the operating system under the release service for three and a half years, and nothing on it says anybody decided that was acceptable.`,
          why:`A risk acceptance is the right document to look for. The unsupported, unpatched component is the finding either way.`},
        {t:`Leave it as a known IT issue while the service keeps running.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It keeps running. It'll go on running until somebody finds a weakness in it that nobody will ever fix, because the only company that could stopped trying three and a half years ago.`,
          why:`Unsupported components can't be patched, so known weaknesses stay open. Running without failure isn't evidence the risk is controlled.`},
        {t:`Put the breach down to the release service's unsupported servers.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He has an old operating system and a breach, and nothing yet that joins them. Halverson hasn't shown how anyone got in, and neither can he.`,
          why:`A weakness shows what was possible. Linking it to the event needs evidence.`},
        {t:`Note the release service runs on unsupported, unpatched software.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`On the back of his visitor's badge first, and in the file later. The service that sends Meridian's money to the Federal Reserve runs on an operating system its maker stopped supporting in October 2023. Nobody bought extended support, and no patch has gone on since. Nothing on the sheet says anyone at Meridian decided to live with that. A component that can't be fixed is a risk somebody has to own, and here nobody does.`,
          why:`Every component needs vendor support or a formally accepted risk with compensating controls. An unsupported, unpatched operating system under a critical service, with no documented decision, is a significant weakness.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s8`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4A9`, title:`Two clicks`, q:`Leonard offers to show DOME's rule history tonight, or lend a read only login. How should Henry answer?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At half past eleven the release ran. On the wall one tile went amber for forty seconds while the files went out to the Fed, and then went green again. A supervisor at the main desk watched the totals come back and initialed a printout. Nobody sat at SR-N1.`,`At ten to twelve Leonard came and stood beside him at the wall.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Leonard">"I hear you asked for the rule history and they told you Friday."</span> He nodded at the tiles. <span class="said" data-who="Leonard">"It's two clicks from here. I can put it up now. Or there's the read only login my team uses. It's in the night lead's drawer. You'd have your answer tonight."</span>`,`Dot had had a login here once, with her own name on it. Somebody had used it at twelve minutes past three on the morning she died, and he'd spent a fortnight asking who.`,(G.trustedia?`On the ninth Henry had let Leonard's report stand in for his own testing. Leonard had meant well then too.`:`Leonard meant it kindly. Leonard generally did.`),`At the back of the room the lawyer from Meridian's legal team looked up from the laptop and then, carefully, down again.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Take the read only login and look at the history himself.`, s:0, flag:`loggedin`, d:{trust:-5,conf:0,health:-6},
          after:`He waits until the room thins out, and then it takes four minutes. The rule history is there, and it's what he hoped it would be, and he has seen it on Meridian's system, under a login Meridian handed him, with no copy, no hash, and his own session now written into DOME's audit trail, beside the records he came to test. Whatever he found, defense counsel will ask how he came to be inside it.`,
          why:`Using the auditee's systems yourself, even read only and even when invited, puts you inside the evidence, outside your authorization and the agreed channel. Obtain log evidence as preserved, verifiable exports.`},
        {t:`Turn down the login and wait for counsel's history export.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`He says no, and then gives Leonard the reason, because Leonard is owed it. Anything he saw tonight he'd have seen on Meridian's screen, by Meridian's leave, with nothing to show for it in the morning but his own sign in, sitting in the record he came to test. The export is coming. He can wait three days for a copy he can stand behind. Leonard looks at the wall for a while. <span class="said" data-who="Leonard">"Ms. Kessler never touched that drawer. Eight years. She said she'd rather answer for her own name."</span> At 11:58 Henry texts Teresa that he was offered a login and turned it down, so it's on the record before anyone else puts it there. She answers at 12:01. <b>Good. Go home.</b>`,
          why:`Evidence from the auditee's systems should come as preserved exports through the agreed channel. Declining direct access protects the evidence and your independence, and recording the offer keeps the file complete.`},
        {t:`Let Leonard put the history on screen while he only watches.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`He touches nothing, and he sees it. What he saw is now something Meridian's director of internal audit chose to show him, on Meridian's screen, at midnight, with Meridian's lawyer watching, and there's no copy of any of it.`,
          why:`Not using the system yourself is better than signing in. But evidence viewed on the auditee's screen leaves nothing verifiable, and it shows the auditee exactly what you're looking for.`},
        {t:`Photograph the DOME wall on the way out for his own file.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`He agreed this afternoon to take no photographs. And a picture of forty green tiles at midnight proves only that DOME looked calm, which is what it's been saying all along.`,
          why:`Keep to the agreed terms of access. Evidence gathered outside them is tainted, and here it's worthless anyway.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s9`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4A2`, title:`Nobody's desk`, q:`Counsel's batch has the queue log, HR attendance and the asset register. What should Henry make of them?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 7:02 on Wednesday his phone went.`,`<prop type="text" from="Maisie" time="7:02 AM">she kept the river. 45:40. she cut a joke about ozone instead</prop>`,`<prop type="text" from="Maisie" time="7:03 AM">it wasnt a good joke</prop>`,`He wrote <b>Good. Tell her I said so.</b>`,`<prop type="text" from="Maisie" time="7:04 AM">you tell her</prop>`,`He wrote to Laura instead. <b>Maisie says you kept the river. Good.</b> She answered before he'd put the phone down.`,`<prop type="text" from="Laura" time="7:06 AM">45:40. I'm keeping the forty seconds too.</prop>`,`Counsel's overnight batch held the records that say who did things.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Three records, via counsel (extracts)">SETL-SUP queue, disposition log. Wed Mar 3, 7:44 AM: break, settlements of Mar 2, timing difference, carried forward. User: bnyland. Workstation: SR-N1. Thu Mar 4, 7:39 AM: break, settlements of Mar 3, same disposition. User: bnyland. Workstation: SR-N1.<br>Meridian HR, attendance. Beth Nyland, settlement supervisor, day shift, 8 AM to 4:30 PM: approved leave Monday, March 1 to Friday, March 12. Remote access during leave: none.<br>Asset register. SR-N1: shared workstation, settlement room, night desk. Assigned to: none. Custodian: none named. Last inventory check: June 2024.</prop>`,`He'd stood three yards from SR-N1 the night before, and nobody had sat at it. Seven forty four and seven thirty nine. He'd had those times since the twelfth. Both were before her shift would have started, if she'd been there at all.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Name Beth Nyland as the preparer from her user ID on both.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`It's her user ID on both, and she was on leave for both, with no remote access, and the machine they came from belongs to nobody. He'd be naming the one person the records say wasn't there.`,
          why:`A user ID shows which account acted, not who used it. When other evidence contradicts the attribution, report what each record shows.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel who was rostered at the night desk those mornings.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's a fair question, and the roster will narrow it. It won't name anyone, because a shared machine with no custodian records nobody, and that's the finding he hasn't written.`,
          why:`The roster is useful corroboration. The weakness is a shared, unassigned workstation and an account that acted while its owner was away.`},
        {t:`Read her leave record against the dispositions made in her name.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`So B.N. is Beth Nyland, a day shift supervisor, and on the mornings of March 3 and 4 she was on approved leave with no remote access. Her user ID carried both breaks forward anyway, from SR-N1, a shared machine at the night desk that the asset register gives to nobody and nobody has counted since June 2024. Her name goes into the finding as the person whose ID was used, and the same sentence says the records show she wasn't the one using it. Then the queue log, the leave record, and the register entry go to Giles and Teresa as a possible irregularity, with nothing added to them. The box he'd drawn on Friday was wide enough for the name. It wasn't wide enough for the sentence that had to follow it.`,
          why:`Every asset needs a named owner and periodic verification, and shared, unassigned machines remove accountability. When an account acts while its owner is absent, report the facts to the appropriate party without assuming who was responsible.`},
        {t:`Go back to the settlement floor tonight and look at SR-N1 himself.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The visit was last night, on terms, and it's over. Tonight he'd be an insurer's investigator asking to sit at a workstation that may be evidence in a federal case.`,
          why:`Physical evidence is examined by those authorized to do it, through the proper channel. Going back alone risks the evidence and your standing.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s10`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4A9`, title:`The same minute`, q:`DOME's SC-04 history came early, logged in Mountain time. What should Henry do with it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const P=s.picks||{},lg=!!(s.flags.loggedin||G.loggedin);
        return [(P[7]===1
          ?`DOME's export had come at ten past seven, three days early. Leonard had walked it through Meridian's counsel himself, the covering note said, at six that morning.`
          :`DOME's export had come at ten past seven, three days early, through Meridian's counsel.`),
        ...(lg?[`It had a second page. At 12:41 AM that morning the administrator audit trail had recorded a sign in under AUDIT_RO, internal audit's shared login, and four minutes of reading. He knew whose they were.`]:[]),
        ...(G.trusteddome?[`First he found the line he'd written on Friday, <b>no production changes before the event (DOME)</b>, with the deploy log marked as a probable error beside it. He left it where it was and read on.`]:[]),
        `<prop type="doc" title="DOME alert rule history, rule SC-04, Settlement components: change outside a declared release window, January 1 to March 9 (extract, Denver time as exported)">58 entries: release window opened by pellridge-deploy, reason a change ticket number; window closed 12 to 41 minutes later.<br>2027-02-26 23:14 MST: rule DISABLED. User: pellridge-deploy. Reason: blank.<br>2027-03-08 09:02 MST: rule ENABLED. User: pellridge-deploy. Reason: CHG-2207.<br>Clock source, DOME and the deployment tool: Meridian time server.</prop>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Report that Pellridge switched the rule off to hide the release.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`It's what the records suggest to anyone in a hurry, and it's a statement about somebody's purpose. Six people share that account, and he can't yet say which of them did anything, let alone why.`,
          why:`Report what the evidence shows. Intent needs evidence of its own, and a shared account can't even identify the person.`},
        {t:`Treat the entry as a release window somebody forgot to close.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Fifty eight windows were opened with a ticket number and closed within the hour. This was never opened as a window at all. The rule was switched off, with no reason given, and it stayed off for nine days.`,
          why:`Don't explain away an exception that breaks the established pattern. Investigate it against the other records.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel who at Pellridge used the account that night.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Teresa asks. Nobody will be able to say from the tool's records, because six people share the account. And the match in front of him needs no name to be a finding.`,
          why:`The question is worth asking. The finding, a monitoring rule disabled without authorization by the account it was meant to watch, stands without it.`},
        {t:`Convert both logs to UTC on a common clock and line them up.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:7},
          after:`The deploy log writes UTC and DOME exports Denver time, so both go into UTC before he lets himself compare them, and he checks that both take their time from the same server, which they do. Then 06:14 and 06:14. In the same minute the unticketed 4.11.2 went into the release service, the same shared account switched off the rule meant to notice changes like it. Fifty eight times before, that account had opened a window with a ticket number and closed it inside the hour. This time it gave no reason, and the rule stayed off for nine days, nine hours and forty eight minutes, until the Monday the late ticket was raised. He records what the logs show and nothing about why. The people being watched held the switch.`,
          why:`Correlating logs needs one time zone and synchronized clocks, so check both before comparing. Monitoring should be independent of what it monitors, and changes to it must be authorized and logged. A rule disabled without a ticket by the account it watches is a serious finding in itself.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s11`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4A7`, title:`Snow`, q:`Agent Obi wants everything on the three service desk tickets, in order and sourced. What should Henry do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const lg=!!(s.flags.loggedin||G.loggedin),tipped=!!G.tippedmeridian;
        return [`It started snowing a little after one, the wet late kind that doesn't settle on the road. At 2:40 Teresa wrote that she'd read the morning's batch herself, as she read everything, and had sent the bureau the queue log, the leave record, and DOME's rule history under a cover letter of her own.`,`At 3:01 the street door went, and then the stairs.`,`Agent Obi didn't take her coat off. She stood at the whiteboard and read it top to bottom, shopping list included, as she had the first time.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"You're still out of coffee filters."</span> Then, before he could answer, <span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"Ms. Nyland is ours to talk to. Not yours."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"I wasn't going to."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"I know. I'm saying it so it's been said."</span>`,
        ...(tipped?[`<span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"And this time I heard it from your lawyer before I heard it from Meridian. Keep that up."</span>`]:[]),
        ...(lg?[`<span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"Meridian's lawyer rang me at noon. There's a session in DOME's audit trail at 12:41 this morning, under internal audit's login, on the same export as the rule change. Defense counsel will read that line before they read the rule."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"I didn't change anything."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"Nobody's going to ask whether you changed anything. They'll ask why you were in there."</span>`]:[]),
        `She wanted one thing more: everything he had on the three service desk tickets from the first week of March, in order, with where each one came from. Then she went.`,`He watched from the window. Her car was across Wazee, outside the brewery, and before she got in she cleared the windshield with her forearm in two strokes. The snow on it had been an inch deep and smooth. It had been snowing since about one, and Teresa's letter had gone at twenty to three. He looked at the bare glass as she pulled away, and at the time on Teresa's email, and didn't write anything down about either.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Set the tickets beside the release and the rule on one time line.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`One page, in time order, each line with its source and nothing in between. Friday, February 26, 11:14 PM: release 4.11.2, no ticket (deploy log). Same minute: rule SC-04 disabled, no reason (DOME). March 2, 3, and 4: approvers report files showing as approved before they'd approved them, each closed in minutes as no fault found (service desk). March 3 and 4: breaks carried forward under the ID of a supervisor on leave (queue log, HR). March 8: late ticket raised, rule switched back on (both). It's the problem record nobody at Meridian opened. It goes to Teresa for the bureau at 4:30, with Giles copied, and at the bottom it says the order of events is established and the cause isn't.`,
          why:`Problem management links recurring incidents to an underlying cause, and the first place to look is recent change. Setting linked records out in time order, with sources and no conclusions about cause or intent, gives the appropriate parties facts they can act on.`},
        {t:`Send her the three tickets as they are for the bureau to join up.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's honest, and it's slow. The bureau will get three tickets with nothing to say they belong to the same week as an unticketed release and a switched off rule, and it'll have to find that out for itself.`,
          why:`Sending the records is right. Linking related incidents to the change and monitoring evidence, as facts in order, is what problem analysis is for.`},
        {t:`Tell her the tickets prove the release broke dual authorization.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`They prove three approvers complained. What the release did, if anything, is in a package nobody independent has examined.`,
          why:`Incidents close to a change suggest a link. Proving cause needs evidence about the change itself.`},
        {t:`Ask Meridian, through counsel, to open a problem record now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`He'd be telling the insured which tickets and which release the federal investigators are now interested in, and asking it to investigate itself.`,
          why:`Don't alert the auditee to the direction of an investigation into possible wrongdoing. Fixing its own process is its business, later.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s12`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`, task:`4A4`, title:`What it held`, q:`The raw event export for February 27 UTC sits beside page 52's timeline. What should Henry do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const P=s.picks||{};
        return [`The raw events came at five: Meridian's export of everything the release service had sent DOME on February 27, UTC, the day the rule went dark. ${P[5]===0?`It came with a hash list and a letter saying the originals were held, which was what Teresa had asked for.`:`It came as a file attached to an email, with nothing to say what else had been kept, and he logged it exactly as received.`}`,`<prop type="doc" title="DOME log feed, release service to DOME, February 27 (UTC) (extract)">Events sent by the release service: 41,288. Events received and stored by DOME: 41,288. Feed gaps: none.<br>06:14:22 UTC. Source: release service. Event: DEPLOY. Package: 4.11.2. Account: pellridge-deploy. Stored: yes. Alert raised: no (rule SC-04 disabled).</prop>`,`Page 52 of the claim pack was still on the desk under a coffee ring, where it had been since Friday. <b>Change monitoring, settlement components, February 1 to March 4: no production changes detected.</b>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Accept the timeline's "no changes detected" as fair wording.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`It's a defensible reading of one word, and every timeline in the claim rests on it. DOME held the release, with its package number and the account that made it, and the claim says DOME detected nothing.`,
          why:`A statement that's narrowly true but leaves out what the records hold is misleading. Report what the source actually contains.`},
        {t:`Check if DOME held the release or only failed to alert.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`The feed between the release service and DOME did its job. Every event sent that day arrived, by count, and the release at 06:14 sits in DOME's store with its package and its account. DOME had the data. What was off was the rule that turns data into an alert, and the claim's timeline was drawn from alerts. So "no production changes detected" is true in the narrowest sense the words allow, and it's the sentence the whole timeline stands on. The finding has two halves and both go in: what DOME held, and what it had been stopped from saying.`,
          why:`Interface controls such as matching record counts show data passed completely. Monitoring output is only as good as the rules applied to it, so a report built on alerts must be tested against the underlying data before anyone relies on it.`},
        {t:`Ask Halverson to rebuild the timeline from the raw events.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`A timeline built from the raw events is what's needed. But Halverson built the first one, for Meridian, from alerts, and he'd be asking it to mark its own work.`,
          why:`Rebuilding from the underlying data is right, but the work should be independent of the original preparer and of the claimant.`},
        {t:`Tell Giles that Halverson's timeline was written to mislead.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`Halverson drew a timeline from DOME's alerts, which is where timelines usually come from. Whether anybody meant it to mislead is a claim about people, and he has no evidence for it.`,
          why:`Report the facts and their effect. Allegations of intent about another firm need evidence, and they aren't the investigator's to make.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f8s10`, title:`Nine days dark`,
     setup:[`DOME's rule SC-04 was disabled at 2027-02-27 06:14 UTC and enabled again at 2027-03-08 16:02 UTC. February 2027 has 28 days. The longest of the 58 ticketed release windows lasted 41 minutes. DOME keeps its administrator audit trail for 28 days, rolling. In February, Denver was on standard time, UTC minus 7.`],
     fields:[{label:`Hours the rule was off (one decimal)`,answer:225.8,tol:0.05},{label:`How many times longer than the longest ticketed window (nearest whole number)`,answer:330,tol:1},{label:`Hour the rule was disabled, Denver time, on a 24 hour clock`,answer:23,tol:0},{label:`Days from 06:14 UTC on Tuesday, March 23 until the February 27 entry rolls off`,answer:4,tol:0}],
     solution:[`From 06:14 UTC on February 27 to 06:14 UTC on March 8 is 9 days, because February has 28 days: the 28th, then March 1 to 8. That's 9 × 24 = 216 hours. From 06:14 to 16:02 is another 9 hours 48 minutes, or 9.8 hours. Total 225.8 hours.`,`225.8 hours × 60 = 13,548 minutes. 13,548 ÷ 41 = 330.4, so about 330 times the longest window anybody opened with a ticket.`,`06:14 UTC minus 7 hours is 23:14 the evening before: 11:14 PM on Friday, February 26, in Denver. It's the same minute as release 4.11.2 in the deploy log, once both are in the same zone.`,`February 27 plus 28 days is March 27, so the entry goes at 06:14 UTC on Saturday, March 27, which is 12:14 AM in Denver now that daylight saving has started. From March 23 to March 27 is 4 days. An export promised for Friday would land with less than a day to spare, which is why preservation is asked for in writing, not waited for.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f8s1`, title:`What kind of ticket?`, intro:`Dot kept a page headed "Fix it, or find out why?" Sort each of these.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`An approver can't open the release screen at 8 AM. The desk restarts the service and it works again.`, choices:[`Incident`,`Problem`,`Known error`,`Change`], answer:0, why:`Service interrupted, service restored. An incident.`},
      {prompt:`The same release screen fault has been logged eleven times this month, and a team is assigned to find out why.`, choices:[`Incident`,`Problem`,`Known error`,`Change`], answer:1, why:`The unknown cause behind repeated incidents. A problem.`},
      {prompt:`The cause has been found and a workaround written up for the desk, but the permanent fix isn't ready.`, choices:[`Incident`,`Problem`,`Known error`,`Change`], answer:2, why:`Root cause known, workaround documented, fix pending. A known error.`},
      {prompt:`The permanent fix goes to the change board for approval before it's deployed.`, choices:[`Incident`,`Problem`,`Known error`,`Change`], answer:3, why:`Fixing the cause changes production, so it goes through change management.`},
      {prompt:`Meridian's three tickets from March 2, 3, and 4, the same complaint each morning, looked at together.`, choices:[`Incident`,`Problem`,`Known error`,`Change`], answer:1, why:`Three repeats of one complaint, cause unknown. They should have been a problem record.`},
      {prompt:`A disk in a server fails overnight and the engineer swaps it under the maintenance contract.`, choices:[`Incident`,`Problem`,`Known error`,`Change`], answer:0, why:`A single failure, fixed. An incident, unless it keeps happening.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`An application runs on an operating system whose vendor support ended last year. What is the IS auditor's PRIMARY concern?`, opts:[`The license may need to be renewed`,`New weaknesses won't be patched`,`The system may begin to run slowly`,`Staff may need retraining on it`], a:1, why:`Once support ends, the vendor stops issuing fixes, so weaknesses found afterward stay open. That risk has to be formally accepted or reduced with compensating controls.`, task:`4A1`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Management keeps an unsupported server because its application can't be moved. What should the IS auditor expect to find?`, opts:[`A note about it in the server's change log`,`An extended warranty on all the hardware`,`A signed risk acceptance and mitigations`,`A plan to retrain the server's main users`], a:2, why:`Running unsupported technology can be a fair business decision, but it should be a documented risk acceptance by management, with compensating controls such as isolation and extra monitoring.`, task:`4A1`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Which is the MOST important foundation for IT asset management?`, opts:[`A barcode tag on every laptop`,`A discovery tool on the network`,`A fixed replacement cycle`,`A full inventory with named owners`], a:3, why:`Everything else, from licensing and patching to monitoring and disposal, depends on knowing what assets exist and who is accountable for each.`, task:`4A2`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`An IS auditor wants to test whether the asset inventory is complete. Which approach is BEST?`, opts:[`Trace devices on the network to the list`,`Trace items on the inventory to the floor`,`Check the date the inventory was approved`,`Ask each owner to confirm their own items`], a:0, why:`Starting from what actually exists and looking for it in the inventory finds assets that are missing from it. Tracing from the list only confirms that listed items exist.`, task:`4A2`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Which control BEST shows that every record in a file sent from one system arrived at the next?`, opts:[`Encrypting the file in transit`,`A nightly backup of both systems`,`Record counts and control totals`,`A log of who sent the file`], a:2, why:`Counts and totals sent with the file and checked on arrival show nothing was lost or added. Encryption protects confidentiality, not completeness.`, task:`4A4`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`An auditor finds a nightly feed of payment files to a vendor that isn't in the interface inventory. What is the MAIN risk?`, opts:[`The vendor may raise its storage fees`,`The feed may slow the nightly batch`,`The inventory's format is out of date`,`Data leaves with no owner or controls`], a:3, why:`An undocumented interface has no owner, no completeness checks, and no oversight, so data can leave or be altered without anyone noticing.`, task:`4A4`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`What is the PRIMARY risk when a key control depends on a spreadsheet its users built?`, opts:[`It lacks access and change controls`,`It can't hold enough rows of data`,`Users may dislike its layout`,`Each user needs a license for it`], a:0, why:`User built tools usually sit outside IT's general controls: no restricted access, change or version control, testing, or reliable backup, so errors and unauthorized changes go unnoticed.`, task:`4A5`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`What should an organization do FIRST to manage end user computing risk?`, opts:[`Ban user built spreadsheets`,`Move them all into the cloud`,`Inventory and risk rate them`,`Train users in formulas`], a:2, why:`You can't control what you don't know about. A risk rated inventory shows which tools matter most, so controls can be applied in proportion.`, task:`4A5`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Which information is MOST useful for planning IT capacity?`, opts:[`The vendor's published rated maximum`,`Use trends and business forecasts`,`Last year's total hardware budget`,`The number of calls to the help desk`], a:1, why:`Capacity planning projects future demand from how resources are used now and how the business expects to grow.`, task:`4A6`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`A monitoring platform often exceeds its licensed daily volume and delays processing on those days. What is the IS auditor's MAIN concern?`, opts:[`The license fee may go up next year`,`Storage costs will increase`,`Alerts may be late or missed`,`The vendor may end support`], a:2, why:`When monitoring falls behind, alerts arrive late or not at all, so events can go unnoticed when it matters most.`, task:`4A6`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`What is the MAIN objective of problem management?`, opts:[`To find and remove root causes`,`To restore service as fast as possible`,`To approve changes to production`,`To measure the service desk's speed`], a:0, why:`Problem management looks for the underlying causes of recurring incidents and removes them. Restoring service quickly is incident management's job.`, task:`4A7`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`A service desk closed the same reported fault three times as "no fault found." What should have happened?`, opts:[`Faster closure of the next ticket`,`Retraining for the reporting users`,`A credit from the service vendor`,`A problem record and root cause work`], a:3, why:`Repeated incidents point to an underlying problem. It should be recorded, owned, and investigated, starting with recent changes, and incidents closed only with the user's confirmation.`, task:`4A7`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Why should all systems set their clocks from a common synchronized source?`, opts:[`To make the log files a little smaller`,`So events can be ordered correctly`,`To make logs easier to encrypt`,`So old logs are deleted on schedule`], a:1, why:`Investigations depend on putting events from different systems in order. Unsynchronized clocks make that unreliable.`, task:`4A9`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`During a fraud investigation, an IS auditor needs a monitoring tool's configuration history as evidence that may be used in court. Which approach is BEST?`, opts:[`Signing in to the tool to view it`,`Having an administrator show it on screen`,`A preserved export with a hash`,`A screenshot sent by the vendor`], a:2, why:`A preserved export with integrity controls, obtained through the agreed channel, gives verifiable evidence and keeps the auditor out of the system being examined.`, task:`4A9`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`Which is the BEST evidence that an outsourced service met its SLA?`, opts:[`The vendor's monthly summary`,`A letter from the vendor`,`No user complaints this year`,`Data the customer can verify`], a:3, why:`Performance should be measured with data the customer can check, against agreed definitions. A vendor's own summary is the weakest of these.`, task:`4A10`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`A build running in production isn't in the controlled library. What does this MOST likely indicate?`, opts:[`It was released outside the process`,`The library has run out of storage`,`The developers forgot to label it`,`Testing took longer than planned`], a:0, why:`Production should be deployed only from the controlled library, so a build missing from it went in outside release controls. Review of chapter 6.`, task:`3B2`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`Who should lead a post implementation review?`, opts:[`The vendor that built it`,`The project's own manager`,`Someone independent of it`,`The system's main users`], a:2, why:`A PIR should be led by someone independent of the project, so the people who built and ran it aren't judging their own work. Review of chapter 6.`, task:`3B4`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{const P=s.picks||{},named=P[3]===0||P[8]===0,lg=!!(s.flags.loggedin||G.loggedin),ar=!!G.acceptedrestore,dome=!!G.trusteddome;
    return [
    ...(lg
      ?[`At six Teresa rang, which she did only when something needed saying in a voice. Meridian's general counsel had written to object to the insurer's investigator's unsupervised access to DOME, and to reserve Meridian's position on everything he'd found since midnight.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Teresa">"I'll answer it. You'll write me a page tonight on exactly what you looked at, and when. Exactly."</span>`,`He wrote it before anything else, and it took an hour, because exactly took that long.`]
      :[]),
    `He sent Giles the two days at seven, one in the morning in London: ${named?`B.N.'s name, which he'd written into the finding as the preparer, and the leave record that meant he had to take it out again, dated, with the first entry left where it was`:`B.N.'s name, and why it was the wrong name to hang anything on`}; DOME's rule, switched off in the minute the release went in; and the store that held everything the rule hadn't been allowed to mention.${dome?` Beside his Friday note about DOME he filed the correction, dated, and left the note where it was.`:``}${ar?(dome?` The correction about November's recovery test went in behind it, so the two would be read together.`:` He sent the November correction again, beside tonight's, so the two would be read together.`):``}`,
    `Then he put his coat on and walked up to the Capitol, which he hadn't done since the morning he'd counted the steps. The snow had stopped. The streets were wet and nearly empty, and the dome was lit, gold against low cloud, as if somebody had been told to keep an eye on the whole of Colfax and had taken it seriously.`,
    `All fortnight he'd thought it was a question of which dome. The one across town had hung on Meridian's wall with every tile green while it held the whole night of February 26 in its store, and said nothing, because the people it watched had told it not to. It hadn't been blind.`,
    `A guard came out of a side door with a flashlight, looked at him for a moment, decided he was harmless, and went back in.`,
    `He took the long way home, along Colfax and past the diner. The lights were still on. The booth by the window was empty, set for the morning with two mugs, and both of them were the right way up.`
  ]},
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch08.mp3`, len:`about 45 minutes`},
  next:`The Tunnel`
});
